Neon = database hosting only.
It gives you a managed/serverless PostgreSQL database with features like autoscaling, connection pooling, scale-to-zero, and database branching. It is best when your backend already exists separately, like your PipelineIQ FastAPI backend on Render. Neon’s main job is: “host my Postgres database reliably.”

Supabase = full backend platform built around Postgres.
It gives you Postgres plus extra app features: Auth, instant APIs, Realtime, Storage, Edge Functions, vector support, and row-level security workflows. It is closer to a Firebase alternative, but with SQL/Postgres.

Simple comparison:

Feature	Supabase	Neon
Core thing	Backend-as-a-service	Serverless Postgres
Database	PostgreSQL	PostgreSQL
Auth included	Yes	No
File storage included	Yes	No
Auto REST APIs	Yes	No
Realtime subscriptions	Yes	No
Edge/serverless functions	Yes	No
DB branching	Some support exists, but not the main identity	Very strong focus
Best for	Apps needing auth + DB + storage quickly	Apps with custom backend needing only Postgres
Example from your projects	GitDock metadata/tokens	PipelineIQ production DB

For GitDock, Supabase made sense because you needed more app-like backend features: storing Google Drive tokens, user/repo metadata, maybe collaborators, permissions, and later storage/auth-related flows.

For PipelineIQ, Neon makes more sense because your backend is already FastAPI on Render. You do not need Supabase Auth or Storage there.
